      "plain_node_ranvier_explanation": "Channels in these gaps help renew a spike. Electrical activity spreads between the gaps under the myelin.",
      "plain_node_ranvier_idea": "Small gaps between myelin layers help renew the electrical spike along an axon.",
      "plain_node_ranvier_question": "A spike travels along an insulated axon and is renewed at its gaps. What do the gaps contribute?",
      "plain_optic_chiasm_visual_path_connection": "After the crossing, each side's main pathway carries information about the opposite side of visual space.",
      "plain_optic_chiasm_visual_path_example": "Information from both eyes is reorganized as it passes this junction.",
      "plain_optic_chiasm_visual_path_explanation": "Only some fibers cross. This contributes to the later organization of visual space across the two sides.",
      "plain_optic_chiasm_visual_path_idea": "A junction where some visual nerve fibers cross to the other side.",
      "plain_optic_chiasm_visual_path_question": "Some fibers cross while others stay on their side. What does this help organize?",
      "plain_optic_nerve_visual_connection": "The signal pathway continues through additional structures; the nerve alone does not recognize objects.",
      "plain_optic_nerve_visual_example": "Signals about a ball leave each eye along its optic nerve.",
      "plain_optic_nerve_visual_explanation": "An optic nerve carries signals from one eye. Further processing happens along connected pathways.",
      "plain_optic_nerve_visual_idea": "Carries nerve signals from one eye toward the brain.",
      "plain_optic_nerve_visual_question": "Signals have left the retina and are traveling toward the brain. Which contribution is highlighted?",
      "plain_rem_stage_connection": "Dreaming can also occur outside REM; sleep phases are identified from several recording features.",
      "plain_rem_stage_example": "During REM sleep, vivid dreaming can occur while many muscles are less active.",
      "plain_rem_stage_explanation": "REM has a characteristic combination of features. An active-looking brain pattern does not by itself mean wakefulness.",
      "plain_rem_stage_idea": "A sleep phase with rapid eye movements and active brain patterns.",
      "plain_rem_stage_question": "A sleeper has rapid eye movements and active brain patterns. What can this describe?",
      "plain_retina_visual_connection": "Seeing also requires pathways and brain networks that process those signals.",
      "plain_retina_visual_example": "Light from a bright ball reaches the retina.",
      "plain_retina_visual_explanation": "The retina begins converting light into signals. Recognizing and responding involve wider networks.",
      "plain_retina_visual_idea": "Light-sensitive cells at the back of the eye begin turning light into nerve signals.",
      "plain_retina_visual_question": "Light from a lamp reaches the back of an eye. Which contribution starts here?",
      "plain_sleep_architecture_decoder_sleep_connection": "A teaching chart is an example; real sleep patterns vary with age and circumstances.",
      "plain_sleep_architecture_decoder_sleep_example": "A sleep chart can show several cycles across one night.",
      "plain_sleep_architecture_decoder_sleep_explanation": "Sleep includes changing stages. Their timing and proportions vary, so a sample chart is not a personal diagnosis.",
      "plain_sleep_architecture_decoder_sleep_idea": "A night of sleep includes repeated changes between sleep stages.",
      "plain_sleep_architecture_decoder_sleep_question": "A chart shows sleep stages changing across a night. How should it be read?",
      "plain_sleep_note": "Sleep stages describe patterns over time, not separate places in the brain. This diagram is a teaching example, not a personal sleep assessment.",
      "plain_soma_connection": "The cell body supports the whole cell, including its branches and axon.",
      "plain_soma_example": "A working nerve cell needs energy and new proteins while it sends and receives signals.",
      "plain_soma_explanation": "The cell body supports the cell's ongoing work. Signaling also depends on the axon, dendrites, and connections.",
      "plain_soma_idea": "The cell body contains the nucleus and machinery that keeps a nerve cell working.",
      "plain_soma_question": "A neuron needs to make proteins and maintain its internal parts. Which contribution is highlighted?",
      "plain_spike_cycle_decoder_neuron_connection": "The teaching curve simplifies real spikes; exact voltages and timing vary.",
      "plain_spike_cycle_decoder_neuron_example": "A neuron can send a series of spikes while information is being processed.",
      "plain_spike_cycle_decoder_neuron_explanation": "The curve shows changing electrical voltage. The cell then recovers so it can support later activity.",
      "plain_spike_cycle_decoder_neuron_idea": "A spike is a brief change in a nerve cell's electrical state, followed by recovery.",
      "plain_spike_cycle_decoder_neuron_question": "A model shows a spike rise, fall, and return toward rest. What is being modeled?",
      "plain_theta_wave_connection": "Theta also appears in other settings; it is not a unique sign of a thought or ability.",
      "plain_theta_wave_example": "Theta activity can appear as a person becomes drowsy.",
      "plain_theta_wave_explanation": "Theta is a rhythm range seen in several contexts. A frequency alone does not identify a particular thought.",
      "plain_theta_wave_idea": "Theta names a range of rhythms slower than alpha and faster than delta.",
      "plain_theta_wave_question": "A drowsy person's recording contains theta activity. What can we conclude?",
      "plain_v1_visual_path_connection": "Other visual areas help with recognizing objects and using what is seen.",
      "plain_v1_visual_path_example": "Signals from the eyes help this area process edges and patterns in a scene.",
      "plain_v1_visual_path_explanation": "Primary visual cortex contributes to early visual processing. Recognizing a familiar object involves additional areas.",
      "plain_v1_visual_path_idea": "An early visual area in the back of the brain that processes information about visual features.",
      "plain_v1_visual_path_question": "A learner sees edges in a picture and recognizes a familiar object. Which explanation fits?",
