      "keyframes_rrcardflip_from_transform_ro": "@keyframes rrCardFlip { from{transform:rotateY(90deg);opacity:0} to{transform:rotateY(0);opacity:1} }",
      "kids_on_bikes": "Kids on bikes",
      "kinetic_content": "A moving car stores energy — kinetic energy. KE = ½mv². Notice the v-squared again. At 30 mph a car has some kinetic energy. At 60 mph it has FOUR TIMES that. At 90 mph it has NINE TIMES. Every bit of that has to go somewhere in a crash — into crumple zones, airbags, and unfortunately, into people. This is the single biggest reason speed limits exist. Modern crumple zones are designed for ~35 mph impacts. Above that, the occupants start to see forces that human bodies cannot survive. Seatbelts, airbags, and crumple zones together save many lives at legal speeds — but they cannot fight physics forever.",
      "kinetic_energy_at_impact": "Kinetic energy at impact",
      "know_the_rubric_free_explore_until_eve": "Know the rubric. Free explore until every maneuver is automatic. Get your certificate.",
      "know_your_car": "Know Your Car",
