      "never": "✗ Never: ",
      "never_pass_a_plow_on_the_right_the_bla": "Never pass a plow on the right — the blade throws snow and debris that direction. Stay 4+ truck lengths back. Plows often travel 30–40 mph even on highways; that slow-moving orange light is your safest speed too.",
      "never_say_these_words": "Never Say These Words",
      "never_swerve_1000_lb_rollover": " — never swerve (1000 lb + rollover)",
      "new": "↺ New",
      "next_24_hours": "Next 24 Hours",
      "nhtsa_data": "⚠️ NHTSA data",
      "night_content": "At night your low beams let you see clearly only about 250 feet ahead (Maine driver's manual). High beams reach about twice as far, ~500 feet. On dry pavement your braking distance at 50 mph is about 116 ft, and at night you should budget a 2-second reaction (slower than the 1.5 s daytime figure, because an unlit hazard has to resolve out of darkness before you even recognize it) — that is another ~147 ft, so roughly 263 ft total. That is already MORE than your low beams show, on a dry road with good tires. The manual says it plainly: faster than about 50 mph on a dark road, you are really 'driving blind'. This is called 'overdriving your headlights' and it is how single-vehicle-at-night crashes happen. Rule: drive slow enough that you could stop within the distance you can see. Use high beams only when clear; Maine §2067 requires dimming within 500 ft of oncoming traffic and within 300 ft when following another vehicle.",
      "night_desc": "Suburban roads after dark. Headlights reach ~250 ft with lows, 500 ft with highs. Do not overdrive them.",
