      "insp2_d11_c1": "General Requirements",
      "insp2_d11_c10_body": "Connections in round and box tubes (pipe and HSS), with their own design, qualification and inspection rules.",
      "insp2_d11_c11": "Strengthening and Repair of Existing Structures",
      "insp2_d11_c1_body": "Scope and limits of the code, and the responsibilities of the Engineer, the Contractor and the Inspector.",
      "insp2_d11_c2": "Normative References",
      "insp2_d11_c2_body": "The other standards the code calls on, such as ASTM steel specifications and AWS filler-metal specifications.",
      "insp2_d11_c3": "Terms and Definitions",
      "insp2_d11_c3_body": "What words like \"effective throat\", \"prequalified\" and \"Engineer\" mean in this code.",
      "insp2_d11_c5": "Prequalification of WPSs",
      "insp2_d11_c5_body": "Joint details, base metals, filler metals and minimum preheats that are exempt from WPS qualification testing when every condition is met. The WPS must still be written, and welders must still be qualified.",
      "insp2_d11_c8_body": "Inspector qualification, inspection of WPSs, welders and work, and acceptance criteria: the visual acceptance table (Table 8.1) plus the criteria for MT, PT, UT and RT.",
      "insp2_d11_full": "AWS D1.1/D1.1M — Structural Welding Code — Steel",
      "insp2_d11_note": "Clause numbers follow AWS D1.1:2020. Editions up to 2015 numbered them differently (Inspection was Clause 6, for example), so always check the edition your contract names. Statically and cyclically loaded requirements are parts within clauses such as Design and Inspection, not clauses of their own.",
      "insp2_d11_scope": "Welded steel structures: buildings, towers, sign and lighting supports, crane runways, offshore platforms and industrial frames, in carbon and low-alloy steel at least 1/8 in thick. (US highway bridges use the companion Bridge Welding Code, AASHTO/AWS D1.5.)",
      "insp2_d11_who": "Structural steel fabricators and erectors, building contractors, welding inspectors, and anyone bidding structural steel work. Navy shipyards such as Bath Iron Works weld hulls mainly to Navy (NAVSEA) welding specifications rather than D1.1, though the skills transfer.",
      "insp2_d11_why": "One of the most widely used welding codes in the US. Building inspectors, structural engineers and fabrication shops all reference it. If you weld steel that holds up a building or a tower, it is usually the code named in the contract.",
      "insp2_d_mt": "MT: flux leakage",
      "insp2_d_pt": "PT: the five steps",
      "insp2_d_rt": "RT: beam angle",
      "insp2_d_ut": "UT: A-scan",
