      "insp2_mt_across": "Field across the crack",
      "insp2_mt_along": "Field along the crack",
      "insp2_mt_aria_across": "MT demo: field lines run between the yoke poles and jump over a crack that crosses them; yellow particles pile up along the crack.",
      "insp2_mt_aria_along": "MT demo: the crack runs parallel to the field lines, which pass it undisturbed; no particles gather.",
      "insp2_mt_aria_none": "MT demo on {value1}: no magnetic field forms in the plate, so no particles gather at the crack.",
      "insp2_mt_cap_across": "The field has to jump the gap, so flux leaks out of the surface and the particles pile up along the crack.",
      "insp2_mt_cap_along": "The field runs parallel to the crack and barely notices it: no leakage, no indication. This is why MT is done in two directions at right angles.",
      "insp2_mt_cap_none": "{value1} is not ferromagnetic: the yoke cannot magnetise it, so nothing gathers at the crack. Switch the material to carbon steel to see MT work.",
      "insp2_mt_dir": "Field direction",
      "insp2_mt_legend": "N, S: yoke poles · white lines: magnetic field · yellow dots: magnetic particles · black line: crack (top view).",
      "insp2_n_mt": "MT — magnetic particle",
      "insp2_n_pt": "PT — liquid penetrant",
      "insp2_n_rt": "RT — radiographic",
      "insp2_n_ut": "UT — ultrasonic",
      "insp2_n_vt": "VT — visual",
      "insp2_ndt_h": "🧭 NDT Method Chooser",
      "insp2_ndt_intro": "Every method has blind spots. Pick the material, the flaw and the access, then see which method finds it and why the others fail. Try the quiz when you can call it without looking.",
      "insp2_next": "Next ➡️",
      "insp2_next_task": "Next task ➡️",
      "insp2_next_weld": "Next weld ➡️",
      "insp2_none_yet": "None yet. An inspector writes down the worst spot before deciding.",
      "insp2_not_yet": "—",
      "insp2_nq1": "A fillet weld on a 304 stainless steel food-processing tank may have a toe crack. Which method fits best?",
      "insp2_nq1_n0": "MT needs a ferromagnetic part; 304 barely magnetises, so no particles gather.",
      "insp2_nq1_n2": "RT can miss a tight crack whose plane is not lined up with the beam, and it is slow and hazardous for a surface flaw.",
      "insp2_nq1_n3": "UT can find cracks, but for a surface crack you can reach, penetrant is simpler and more certain.",
      "insp2_nq1_why": "304 is austenitic stainless, which is not ferromagnetic, so MT cannot work. A toe crack breaks the surface, which is exactly what penetrant finds.",
      "insp2_nq2": "Carbon-steel crane runway fillets need a fast field check for fine toe cracks the eye might miss.",
      "insp2_nq2_n1": "VT always comes first, but a tight fatigue crack can be invisible to the eye; MT reveals it.",
      "insp2_nq2_n2": "RT is slow, needs a radiation exclusion zone, and can miss cracks not lined up with the beam.",
      "insp2_nq2_n3": "UT works but is slower and needs a skilled operator for a surface check MT does in seconds.",
      "insp2_nq2_why": "Carbon steel is ferromagnetic, so an MT yoke finds surface and near-surface cracks quickly. Check in two directions at right angles, because a crack shows best when the field crosses it.",
      "insp2_nq3": "A 1 in carbon-steel CJP butt weld must be checked for internal porosity and slag. Both sides are reachable and the owner wants a permanent image.",
      "insp2_nq3_n0": "PT only finds flaws open to the surface.",
      "insp2_nq3_n1": "MT only reaches near-surface flaws, not the middle of a 1 in weld.",
      "insp2_nq3_n2": "UT can detect them, but rounded pores scatter sound and are harder to size, and plain UT leaves no picture.",
      "insp2_nq3_why": "Porosity and slag are volumetric: they remove metal along any beam direction, so RT shows them clearly, and the film or digital image is a permanent record.",
      "insp2_nq4": "A thick carbon-steel groove weld may have lack of fusion along the bevel face. You can reach only the outside.",
      "insp2_nq4_n0": "RT needs film on the far side, and a flat flaw at the bevel angle is often invisible to a beam not lined up with it.",
      "insp2_nq4_n1": "PT cannot reach a flaw buried in the weld.",
      "insp2_nq4_n3": "VT cannot see inside the weld.",
      "insp2_nq4_why": "Lack of fusion is planar. An angle-beam UT probe can be aimed to meet the bevel face square-on, and it works from one side.",
      "insp2_nq5": "Before welding, you must check an aluminum plate for laminations (internal layers parallel to the surface).",
      "insp2_nq5_n1": "RT looks straight through a lamination: it barely changes the metal thickness along the beam, so it hardly shows.",
      "insp2_nq5_n2": "Aluminum is not ferromagnetic, so MT cannot work.",
      "insp2_nq5_n3": "A lamination inside the plate does not break the surface; PT may show it only where it reaches a cut edge.",
      "insp2_nq5_why": "A lamination faces a straight-beam UT probe square-on, so it reflects strongly, and the echo time tells you its depth.",
      "insp2_nq6": "A carbon-steel weld may have a crack just under the cap, not open to the surface.",
      "insp2_nq6_n0": "Penetrant cannot enter a crack that does not break the surface.",
      "insp2_nq6_n1": "The eye cannot see below the surface.",
      "insp2_nq6_n2": "RT may miss a crack whose plane is not lined up with the beam.",
      "insp2_nq6_why": "MT finds near-surface flaws as well as surface ones: a flaw just below the surface still distorts the field and leaks flux.",
      "insp2_nq7": "Before any other NDT, which method does a CWI apply to every weld to check size, profile, undercut and craters?",
      "insp2_nq7_n0": "UT looks for internal flaws, after the weld has passed visual.",
      "insp2_nq7_n2": "PT shows surface-breaking flaws, but it does not measure size or profile.",
      "insp2_nq7_n3": "RT is for internal flaws and costs far more.",
      "insp2_nq7_why": "Visual inspection comes first on every weld: it is cheap and fast, and it judges size and profile, which no other method is designed to do.",
      "insp2_nq8": "A 304 stainless pipe butt weld must be checked for internal porosity. You can place film inside the pipe.",
      "insp2_nq8_n0": "304 is not ferromagnetic, so MT cannot work.",
      "insp2_nq8_n1": "Austenitic weld metal has coarse grains that scatter and bend sound, so conventional UT is unreliable there.",
      "insp2_nq8_n3": "PT cannot find porosity buried inside the weld.",
      "insp2_nq8_why": "RT is excellent at volumetric flaws like porosity, and it does not care that the metal is austenitic.",
