      "sym2_mq_score": "Score: {value1} / {value2}",
      "sym2_mtn_ext": "Pick a fabrication you have made or seen. Reverse-estimate it with the checked-practice formulas: plate weight, pounds of filler (with deposition efficiency), hours and a bid. Compare with what was actually charged, if you can find out.",
      "sym2_mtn_m1": "\"I can eyeball most measurements\" — most rejected fabrications were eyeballed.",
      "sym2_mtn_m2": "\"Tolerance only matters in precision work\" — structural welding has tolerance too, just looser.",
      "sym2_mtn_m3": "\"The weld weighs what the rod I bought weighs\" — stub ends, slag and spatter mean you buy more than you deposit; stick keeps only about 60% of the electrode weight.",
      "sym2_mtn_m4": "\"The shop rate is the welder's wage\" — the rate also pays for machines, insurance, power and the office.",
      "sym2_mtn_m5": "\"A circle on the line means a plug weld\" — plug/slot is a rectangle; a circle on the line is a spot weld, or a seam weld with two lines through it.",
      "sym2_mtn_q1": "Why are most rejected fabrications measurement errors, not welding errors?",
      "sym2_mtn_q2": "Five 24-3/4 in plates are each cut 1/16 in long. How far off is the assembly, and does it pass a ±1/8 in tolerance?",
      "sym2_mtn_q3": "The filler problem divides by deposition efficiency. Why does a stick welder buy more pounds of rod than a MIG welder buys wire for the same weld?",
      "sym2_mtn_q4": "Bid the same job at $65/h and at $95/h. Which lines of the bid change, and which do not?",
      "sym2_next_ch": "Next challenge →",
