      "sym2_tab_blueprint": "5. Mini Blueprint",
      "sym2_tab_builder": "4. Symbol Builder",
      "sym2_tip_allaround": "A circle at the arrow junction = weld all the way around the joint.",
      "sym2_tip_angle": "The groove angle, in degrees, sits beyond the groove symbol.",
      "sym2_tip_contour": "Contour: a straight line = flush, a curve bulging away = convex, a curve dipping in = concave. None unless the spec asks.",
      "sym2_tip_field": "A flag at the arrow junction = field weld, made on site.",
      "sym2_tip_finish": "The finish letter sits beyond the contour: G = grind, M = machine, C = chip.",
      "sym2_tip_lp": "Length-pitch goes to the RIGHT: weld length, then centre-to-centre spacing. A continuous weld has none.",
      "sym2_tip_root": "The root opening is the gap between the members at the root. It is written inside the groove symbol.",
      "sym2_tip_side": "Weld on the side the arrow touches = symbol BELOW the line. On the far side = ABOVE. Both sides = both.",
      "sym2_tip_size": "Size goes to the LEFT of the symbol: leg size for a fillet, depth of preparation for a groove, hole size for a plug. Leave it off when the spec gives none.",
      "sym2_tip_tail": "The tail carries the process or procedure. Add one only when the spec names it.",
      "sym2_tip_type": "Match the bead in the joint drawing: a triangular bead in a corner is a fillet; a filled groove between plates is a groove weld, named by its edge shape.",
      "sym2_tn_ext": "Find a public-domain fabrication drawing (search \"weld symbol blueprint\"). Rebuild three of its symbols in the Symbol Builder, then write each one as a plain-English work order: weld type, side, size, length, flags and tail.",
      "sym2_tn_m1": "\"The symbol goes on the side of the line where the weld is in the picture\" — position is a code: below = arrow side, above = other side, whichever way the arrow points.",
      "sym2_tn_m2": "\"A square groove is drawn as a square\" — it is two parallel lines. A closed rectangle is the plug/slot symbol.",
      "sym2_tn_m3": "\"A double-V looks like a diamond\" — groove symbols touch the line at the root, so a double-V reads as an X and a double-bevel as a K.",
      "sym2_tn_m4": "\"The vertical leg shows which plate is beveled\" — it is always drawn on the left. The ARROW points to the member that gets cut.",
      "sym2_tn_m5": "\"The tail is optional flavor\" — the tail is where the engineer names the process, the procedure (WPS) and other references.",
      "sym2_tn_q1": "Arrow side is always BELOW the reference line, even when the arrow leaves the right end or points up. Why does a fixed rule beat \"draw it where the weld is\"?",
      "sym2_tn_q2": "In the Symbol Builder, which field did you miss most often? What would help you catch it on a real print?",
      "sym2_tn_q3": "A fillet symbol shows no size. Is that a print error, or does it mean something? (Some general notes set a default size; when in doubt, ask before you weld.)",
      "sym2_tn_q4": "On the mini blueprint, symbol 2 touches the left face but calls for an other-side V. How could the drafter redraw it so the groove is on the arrow side?",
