      "src_link_ncsu_milkweed": "NC State Extension · Common milkweed plant structure",
      "src_link_nrcs_monarch_habitat": "USDA NRCS · Monarch habitat and feeding needs",
      "src_link_nrcs_pollination": "USDA NRCS · How animal pollination works",
      "src_link_ucanr_wing_patterns": "UC Agriculture and Natural Resources · Wing patterns visible before emergence",
      "src_link_umn_chrysalis": "University of Minnesota · A monarch develops inside its chrysalis",
      "src_link_usfs_butterflies": "USDA Forest Service · Butterflies as pollinators",
      "src_link_xerces_nectar_plants": "Xerces Society · Regional nectar plants and milkweed hosts",
      "src_milkweed_visitors": "Wilson · Insect visitors to common milkweed",
      "src_note_diverse_model_scope": "The learner and life-cycle investigation follow the monarch (Danaus plexippus). Adult meadow visitors also include eastern tiger swallowtail, painted lady, and introduced cabbage white. Nectar use does not imply a shared caterpillar host plant; cabbage whites visit milkweed here, while the other visitors can use both flower patches. This summer scene represents a Mid-Atlantic habitat investigation. Plants and wing patterns are illustrative and enlarged. Guided routes, flight speed, distances, and energy are teaching choices, not field measurements. The generation you follow is a teaching model of one outcome, not a population simulation: it turns on whether milkweed is present, and it deliberately leaves out weather, predators, parasites, disease, how many eggs are laid, and how many survive — all of which matter in a real meadow, where most eggs do not reach adulthood even on good milkweed. Real development also takes weeks and depends on temperature; the stages here advance when you choose, in a fixed order. Other butterfly species can have different host plants.",
      "src_note_experiments": "In these experiments you place model eggs on any examined patch to test what happens; real female monarchs choose milkweed. An egg does not feed, so a patch without host leaves first blocks the feeding caterpillar stage. The mowing experiment keeps the chrysalis inside the patch and assumes cutting removes its support. Real caterpillars often leave their milkweed plant to find a secure place to pupate.",
      "src_note_model_scope": "Species: monarch (Danaus plexippus). This summer scene represents a Mid-Atlantic habitat investigation. Plants and wing patterns are illustrative and enlarged. Guided routes, flight speed, distances, and energy are teaching choices, not field measurements. The generation you follow is a teaching model of one outcome, not a population simulation: it turns on whether milkweed is present, and it deliberately leaves out weather, predators, parasites, disease, how many eggs are laid, and how many survive — all of which matter in a real meadow, where most eggs do not reach adulthood even on good milkweed. Real development also takes weeks and depends on temperature; the stages here advance when you choose, in a fixed order. Other butterfly species can have different host plants.",
      "src_summary": "Science notes & sources",
      "ssn_col_mowing": "Mowing",
